<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; background-color: #eaf2f8; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding: 10px 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Liked Your Presentation Because... – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding: 5px 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Books and Movies</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Liked Your Presentation Because...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose short pieces of creative writing. Interactive: Provide feedback on short texts during a peer review session.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose a short, creative piece inspired by a favorite book, using simple details to describe key elements (e.g., "In my version of the story, the animals become friends and start their own adventure"). Can provide feedback on a peer's description of a movie (e.g., "Your story about the movie is interesting"). Can compose a simple review, sharing why a book or movie is enjoyable (e.g., "I read a book about life in the future. It showed the impact of technology in our lives, and robots played an active role").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by projecting images of popular books and movies. Students are asked to choose one they like and quickly write down its title and one adjective to describe it (e.g., "Spider-Man: thrilling," "Harry Potter: magical"). The teacher asks a few students to share their choices and reasons orally, prompting with "Why do you like/prefer this one?" This activates prior knowledge and vocabulary related to the scenario "Books and Movies."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to express preferences and provide reasons, essential for the writing task. For instance, the teacher points to a movie poster and says: "I like watching action movies because they are very *thrilling* and *engaging*." Then points to a book: "I prefer reading fantasy books over documentaries because fantasy stories are more *emotional* and allow me to *relax*." The teacher writes these sentences on the board, highlighting the structure "I like/prefer [verb + -ing/noun] because..." and using target vocabulary (thrilling, engaging, emotional, relax).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of expressing preferences and reasons for writing.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "When I say 'I prefer action movies over comedies,' does it mean I like both equally, or one more than the other?" (Expected answer: "One more than the other.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "What word helps me explain *why* I enjoy a *character* or a *plot*?" (Expected answer: "Because.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher provides students with a short, simple written review of a fictional book or movie. This serves as a model text for their own writing. The text describes a simple plot, a character, and the reviewer's reasons for liking it, using present simple, past simple, and target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Text:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"I read a book called 'The Whispering Forest' last week. It was an *engaging* fantasy *genre*. The main *character*, Elara, was very brave and always wanted to *express* her feelings. The *plot* involved a long journey to find a magical stone. I enjoyed this book because it was very *emotional* and made me *reflect* on friendship. I prefer fantasy stories over documentaries because they are more *entertaining* and creative."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Writing: Can compose a simple review, sharing why a book or movie is enjoyable.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text silently.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks gist questions: "What kind of book did the person read?" "What was the main *theme* of the story?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students complete a True/False exercise based on the text:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The book was about finding a magical tree." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The main *character* was afraid." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The reviewer prefers fantasy because it's *entertaining*." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Present simple to describe preferences and reasons, verb + -ing + noun + because (e.g., "I like reading fiction books because they are exciting."), Past simple (e.g., "I watched a comedy yesterday." "It was about..."), Comparing preferences (e.g., "I prefer action movies over comedies because they are more thrilling."). Vocabulary: movie, book, poetry, character, plot, theme, documentary, presentation, genre, express, describe, relax, watch, enjoy, analyze, reflect, stand, like, prefer, entertaining, engaging, emotional, favorite, first, next, after, finally, creatively, emotionally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with sentences that students must complete using the correct form of verbs and target vocabulary (nouns/adjectives) related to books and movies. This focuses on grammar accuracy and vocabulary recall for writing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Examples:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My *favorite* ______________ is 'Fantasy.' I like ______________ (read) them because they are so ______________ (engage)." (Answers: genre, reading, engaging)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Yesterday, I ______________ (watch) a ______________ (documentary) about space. It was very ______________ (entertain)." (Answers: watched, documentary, entertaining)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I prefer ______________ (movie) over ______________ (book) because movies are more ______________ (thrill)." (Answers: movies, books, thrilling)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Expansion):</b> Students work individually to expand simple sentences into more descriptive ones, incorporating reasons and sequencing words. The teacher provides sentence starters.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Examples:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I like [Book/Movie title]." -> "I like [Book/Movie title] because [reason with adjective]. The main *character* was [adjective]."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The *plot* was interesting." -> "The *plot* was interesting because [detail]. *First*, [event 1]. *Next*, [event 2]."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Check (Sentence Construction):</b> In pairs, students write 3-4 sentences each describing a *character* or a *plot* twist from their *favorite* book or movie. They must use at least two target vocabulary words and one "because" clause. After writing, they exchange their sentences and check their partner's grammar and vocabulary usage, focusing on spelling and sentence structure. They can give simple feedback like "Your sentence about the *character* is good, but check the verb form."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> "Write a Mini-Review & Peer Feedback."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Students will individually compose a short, creative piece of writing (a mini-review/description) about a book or movie. Then, they will provide written feedback on a peer's text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Individual Writing (10 min):</b> Each student chooses a *favorite* book or movie and writes a short paragraph (6-8 sentences) describing it. They must include:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The title and *genre*.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A brief description of the *plot* or a key *character* using past simple.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Reasons why they *enjoy* it, using "I like/prefer... because..." and at least three adjectives (e.g., *entertaining*, *engaging*, *emotional*).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one comparison of preferences (e.g., "I prefer this *genre* over *poetry* because it's more *thrilling*.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Emphasis on using sequencing words (first, next, after, finally) if describing plot.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Peer Feedback (5 min):</b> Students exchange their written mini-reviews with a partner. Each student reads their partner's text and provides constructive feedback in writing, focusing on:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Is the description clear? (e.g., "Your story about the *movie* is interesting.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are the reasons for liking it well-explained? (e.g., "I liked your description because you clearly *express* why it is *emotional*.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are vocabulary words used effectively?</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Teacher reminds students of polite feedback language, e.g., "I think...", "Maybe you could...", "Your idea is good because...").</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will submit their written mini-review along with their peer's written feedback. The teacher will also observe the peer feedback process.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation & Product Review):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion:</b> Can compose a short, creative piece inspired by a favorite book, using simple details to describe key elements. (The description is clear and logical).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Correct use of present simple for preferences and past simple for events/plot.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Incorporates at least 3-4 recommended vocabulary words (e.g., *character, plot, theme, genre, entertaining, engaging, emotional*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Giving Feedback:</b> Can provide constructive feedback on a peer's description (Sociolinguistic: provides constructive feedback).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper before leaving.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What was one new vocabulary word I used in my writing today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Was I able to clearly explain *why* I like my chosen book/movie?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What part of writing about books and movies did I find most challenging today? (e.g., using past simple, remembering vocabulary, organizing my ideas *creatively*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. How can I improve my creative writing for next time, especially when sharing reasons for my preferences?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the writing task worked well? Did students effectively use the target grammar (present simple for preferences, past simple for plot) and vocabulary in their mini-reviews?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face in composing their pieces or in providing constructive feedback to their peers? Were the instructions for *describing* and *expressing* opinions clear?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments (e.g., more scaffolding for sentence structure, explicit modeling of feedback phrases, additional practice with sequencers like *first*, *next*, *after*, *finally*) are needed for future writing lessons or the final project in Lesson 5? Was the pronunciation practice of /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ and vowel sounds like /ʊ/ and /uː/ relevant and effectively integrated during modeling?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The written reviews and the peer feedback students generated in this lesson will be used as a basis for the final "Mediation & Integration" lesson. Students will use their writing to prepare a short oral summary or a visual presentation to share with the class, integrating their written work into a communicative project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>